    faqs: [
      { question: 'Is the Steelcase Leap V2 better than the Herman Miller Aeron?', answer: 'It depends on your priorities. The Leap V2 has 4D armrests, a softer seat, and costs $466 less. The Aeron runs cooler over long sessions, has a more durable mesh seat, and a longer documented track record. Based on feature analysis, the Aeron is preferred for hot climates and 8+ hour days, the Leap V2 for everyone else. For the side-by-side breakdown see our Herman Miller Aeron vs Steelcase Leap V2 comparison.' },
      { question: 'How long does the Steelcase Leap V2 last?', answer: 'Steelcase rates the Leap V2 for "lifetime" use, which is defined as 10 years of single-shift use under the warranty. According to owner reports, Leap V2 chairs remain in active office use for 12 to 15 years with only minor upholstery wear. The mechanism is genuinely overbuilt.' },
      { question: 'Does the Leap V2 come with a headrest?', answer: 'Steelcase offers an optional headrest as a $130 upgrade, available only on the V2 (not the original Leap). According to Steelcase specifications, the headrest is well-padded and adjustable but only reaches users above 5\'8". Shorter users may find it pushes their head forward.' },
      { question: 'Is the Leap V2 good for lower back pain?', answer: 'Yes. The LiveBack technology flexes with your spine as you shift, which reduces the static pressure that causes fatigue. Based on feature analysis, the Leap V2 offers similar relief to the Aeron with PostureFit SL, although the Leap V2\'s lumbar is less adjustable in depth.' },
      { question: 'Where is the cheapest place to buy a Steelcase Leap V2?', answer: 'Crandall Office Furniture sells refurbished Leap V2 chairs for around $549 with a 12-year warranty — roughly half the new price. For new units, Steelcase\'s authorized dealer network runs the same price as buying direct, so buying from whichever dealer offers free shipping is recommended.' },
      { question: 'How does the Leap V2 compare to the Steelcase Gesture?', answer: 'The Gesture is Steelcase\'s newer flagship, designed around constant device use — its armrests rotate 360 degrees to track phones and tablets. The Leap V2 has a softer seat, a more traditional recline, and costs about $200 less. Most buyers who do not work across multiple devices will be happier with the Leap V2; power users who live in video calls and switch devices all day should look at the Gesture. See our Steelcase Gesture review for the full breakdown.' },
      { question: 'Is a refurbished Leap V2 worth buying?', answer: 'Usually yes. Crandall and a few other refurbishers replace the foam, upholstery, and armrest pads with new components, then re-cover the frame. The result is functionally a new chair for around half the price, often with a longer written warranty than Steelcase offers new. The only downside is lead time (3 to 6 weeks) and limited upholstery color choice.' },
      { question: 'Can the Leap V2 support a 300-pound user?', answer: 'Yes. Steelcase rates the Leap V2 to 400 lbs. The base is die-cast aluminum and the gas cylinder is the same heavy-duty unit used in the Aeron. Heavier users should skip the headrest option, which loses rigidity above 250 lbs of downward pressure.' },
    ],
